window.docContents[228].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Constructing-energy-solutions":{"contents":"We start with the Dirichlet problem in the energy space. Let\n be nonempty, bounded and open, with ,\nand let  be an autonomous bounded measurable symmetric uniformly\nelliptic matrix. No regularity of the boundary is required. For initial data\nin  and a locally Lipschitz map\n,\nthe Dirichlet wave semigroup and its Duhamel integral construct a maximal\ncontinuous trajectory . On each compact initial interval\nbefore the lifetime, the trajectory supplies Bochner classes satisfying\n and  weakly,\nwith both prescribed initial values. A scalar term  on the left\ntherefore corresponds to . For a general scalar source, the\nmapping estimates for  are hypotheses. For , they\nare proved when  in dimensions , and for every\nfinite real  in dimensions one and two.\n\nA separate theorem proves uniqueness among weak solutions represented by\ncontinuous curves in this energy space. If  is Lipschitz on every bounded energy ball, a\nfinite maximal lifetime forces the state norm to exceed every bound near\nthat endpoint. Local Lipschitz continuity alone does not give the uniform\nconstants on balls needed for this conclusion in infinite dimensions.\n\nTo see why the uniformity matters, restart the construction.\nWrite  and let  be the linear wave evolution. On a short\ninterval after , the mild equation reads\n\n\n\nIf the state stays in a ball of radius , a Lipschitz bound on a larger\nball makes this integral map a contraction for one common positive time\n, also chosen so the map preserves the ball of candidate\ncurves. Its value does not shrink as  approaches a finite endpoint.\nRestart less than  before that endpoint and uniqueness glues\nthe new solution to the old one, extending the trajectory past it.\n\nAn a priori bound therefore yields global existence when it controls the\nnorm used by the local construction. On a bounded Dirichlet domain,\nPoincaré supplies the  displacement bound from the gradient bound.\nFor the smooth whole-space construction below, a separate tame composition\nestimate turns a uniform displacement bound on a time strip into the higher\nSobolev bound needed for continuation.\n\nThe linear homogeneous wave evolution conserves its quadratic energy;\nconservation of a nonlinear potential energy is a separate statement. The\nenergy identities below concern classical solutions under stated regularity,\nintegrability, and flux hypotheses, and give no critical-power global theory\nfor the energy-space constructor.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations","header":"7.2.4.1. Constructing energy solutions","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Constructing-energy-solutions"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Two-Variable-Canonical-Form--Constructing-coordinates-for-the-full-equation":{"contents":"For variable coefficients, choosing null covectors separately at each point\ndoes not yet produce coordinate functions; the coordinates come from first\nintegrals of the characteristic fields.\n\nSuppose the three principal coefficients are  near  and\n. There is a local coordinate map\n, with open source containing , whose map and\ninverse are . On its source,For the full nondivergence-form operatorput . At each source point where  is , the\nequation  is equivalent toAll coefficients on the right are evaluated at .\nThe drift  and potential  are arbitrary functions; only the principal\ncoefficients carry a regularity hypothesis.\n\nA fixed linear shear provides a noncharacteristic initial line, including\nwhen the original  vanishes. The two characteristic ODEs construct\n first integrals with independent differentials. The inverse function\ntheorem supplies the chart; shrinking its source keeps the mixed coefficient\nnonzero. The second-order chain rule gives the transformed principal terms\nand the additional drift . Dividing by  gives the\nstated equivalence with the full equation.\n\nFor a  characteristic map  and  function\n, set , . With  and  as\nin the local chart theorem,\nthe full operator satisfies the composition identityThe derivatives of  are evaluated in the new coordinates. Thus the\nmixed-to-wave change retains the drift and potential as well as the\nprincipal part.\n\nApply the full second-order composition formula to the characteristic map.\nThe null-coordinate identities eliminate both pure characteristic terms.\nThe sum-and-difference chain rule converts the mixed term into the difference\nof pure second derivatives and converts the two first derivatives into their\nsum and difference. The zeroth-order term is unchanged by composition.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Two-Variable Canonical Form","header":"7.2.2.1. Constructing coordinates for the full equation","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Two-Variable-Canonical-Form--Constructing-coordinates-for-the-full-equation"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Initial-Data-and-Convolution":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel turns an initial profile into a positive-time heat flow by\nspatial convolution. Three facts drive the construction: the kernel is\nnonnegative, it has mass one, and its mass concentrates at the origin as time\napproaches zero. Positivity and unit mass give the  estimate;\nconcentration recovers the initial trace.\n\nFor positive time the same formula is smoother than the original datum.\nDifferentiating the Gaussian under the integral proves the heat equation even\nwhen the initial profile is assumed only bounded and continuous. The argument\nhas four parts: the convolution formula, the domination needed for\ndifferentiation, the PDE residual, and the approximate-identity limit at time\nzero.\n\nFor initial data  and , defineThe total Lean function returns  for .\n\nSince  is a probability measure for each ,\nthe convolution is an average of values of , and an average cannot\nexceed a uniform bound on the datum.\n\nThe whole-space heat evolution preserves constant data. More generally, if\n is continuous and  for every , thenThe totalized Lean formula satisfies the same estimate for  by its\ndefinition. This is the -contraction estimate for the explicit\nheat-kernel evolution, stated pointwise.\n\nFor constant data, pull the constant through the convolution and use unit\nmass. For bounded data, dominate the absolute value of the integrand by\n, integrate, and again use unit mass.\n\nBoundedness of  and unit mass control the size of the evolution but say\nnothing about its derivatives. Those come from the kernel alone: for\n the time derivative of the Gaussian is dominated near  by an\nintegrable majorant, so differentiation passes through the integral even\nthough the datum is merely bounded and continuous.\n\nLet  be continuous with  for\nevery , fix , and let . Then the heat-kernel\nconvolutionis differentiable at , with derivativeThe integrand is the pointwise time derivative of the Gaussian multiplied by\nthe datum, so this is the first \"differentiate under the integral sign\" step\nof Evans's heat initial-value theorem. The statement concerns the convolution\nintegral itself; the totalized initial-value formula agrees with it near every\npositive time, which is how\n inherits this\nderivative.\n\nWork on the time ball . There the coefficient\n is bounded by\n, and the Gaussian  is bounded by\na constant depending on  times . Their\nproduct is therefore dominated by a polynomial in  times the\nslower Gaussian , which is integrable, and\nmultiplying by the global bound  for  keeps it integrable. The\ndominated differentiation theorem for parameter-dependent integrals then\nmoves the time derivative of the Gaussian inside the integral.\n\nThe time derivative is one slot of the first- and second-order calculus\nneeded to evaluate . The same Gaussian majorants control the spatial\ngradient and Hessian, and since the totalized formula agrees with the\nconvolution on a neighbourhood of every positive time, the derivative just\ncomputed transfers to the initial-data convolution unchanged.\n\nLet  be continuous and globally bounded. At every positive-time point,\nthe heat-kernel convolution is differentiable in time and Fréchet\ndifferentiable in space, with both derivatives obtained by differentiating\nthe Gaussian beneath the integral. It is jointly differentiable in\n, every first spatial coordinate derivative is itself spatially\ndifferentiable, and the spatial Laplacian may be moved through the\nconvolution integral.These are dominated-differentiation theorems with explicit integrable\nGaussian majorants. They give the first- and second-order regularity that\nthe classical heat residual needs. Joint  smoothness on the\npositive-time half-space is the joint smoothness theorem.\n\nWork in a positive-time neighbourhood bounded away from zero. Derivatives of\nthe Gaussian are bounded there by a polynomial times a slower Gaussian,\nwhich is integrable and remains an admissible majorant after multiplication\nby the global bound for . Apply the parameter-dependent integral\ntheorems for the time derivative, spatial derivative, and Hessian slots, and\nsum the diagonal Hessian slots to obtain the Laplacian formula.\n\nWith both derivatives expressed as integrals against the datum, the residual\nreduces to an identity between integrands. The time derivative of\n and its Laplacian in  agree pointwise by the kernel\nequation, so their difference integrates to zero against any bounded .\n\nIf  is continuous and bounded, then its heat-kernel evolution satisfiesfor every  and every . The assumptions justify differentiating\nunder the spatial integral.\n\nDifferentiate beneath the integral using Gaussian derivative majorants, then\nintegrate the pointwise heat-kernel identity against the bounded datum.\n\nThe positive-time residual says nothing about . The totalized formula\ntakes the value  there by definition, but that assignment is not a\nlimit statement; recovering the datum from positive times is a separate\napproximate-identity argument, driven by concentration of kernel mass near\nthe origin rather than by any derivative bound.\n\nFor bounded continuous , fix  and . Then there is\n such that, for every  and ,\n\nUse unit mass to subtract g(x_0) inside the convolution.  Split into a near\nregion controlled by continuity and a far Gaussian tail controlled by\nboundedness.\n\nA pointwise solution of the forced heat Cauchy problem has the time and\nspatial differentiability needed to interpret every derivative in the\nequation, satisfies the forced residual at positive time, takes the assigned\nvalue at time zero, and converges locally uniformly to that datum as time\ndecreases to zero.\n\nFor bounded continuous initial data, the convolution formula simultaneously\nsatisfieslocally uniformly as . The conclusion is bundled as a\npointwise-solution structure containing the required derivative hypotheses,\nthe residual equation, the assigned value at zero, and the locally uniform\ntrace.\n\nCombine the positive-time differentiability and residual theorems with the\nlocally uniform initial-trace theorem.\n\nPositivity of the kernel has a qualitative consequence beyond the\n bound. Because  everywhere for , a\nnonnegative datum that is positive at one point contributes a strictly\npositive amount to the convolution at every spatial point, however far that\npoint lies from where the datum is positive.\n\nIf the initial datum is continuous and bounded, is nonnegative everywhere,\nand is strictly positive at some point, thenThus the heat-kernel evolution is immediately positive at every spatial\npoint, even when the initial positivity is localized.\n\nContinuity gives a ball on which the datum has a uniform positive lower bound.\nAt a fixed positive time the Gaussian has a positive lower bound on that\nball, so the convolution has a strictly positive contribution there; the\nremaining integrand is nonnegative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow","header":"6.2.3. Initial Data and Convolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--Initial-Data-and-Convolution"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Integrated-Weak-Evolution--Integrated-weak-solutions":{"contents":"Let . In one Hilbert space , the integrated formulation uses its inner product\nfor both the time term and initial datum. More generally, let\n be a Gelfand triple, let\n be induced by a time-dependent bilinear form, and fix\n and . A Gelfand integrated weak solution is a Bochner\nclass  for which the tested spatial-form and forcing terms are\nintegrable and the following identity holds:This is required for every  and every scalar function\n differentiable at each point of , with\n and . The forcing pairing is integrable by Hölder\nunder the stated  assumption; the definition records integrability of both tested\nterms explicitly.\nBoth versions are invariant under almost-everywhere changes of the forcing or\ntrajectory.\n\nA solution concept stated for Bochner classes should agree with the pointwise\none on trajectories regular enough for both to make sense. In the case\n, integration by parts against a scalar time test provides this\nconsistency check.\n\nSuppose the Hilbert-space weak evolution has the stated time derivative throughout the interval.\nFor the chosen spatial and scalar time tests, assume the derivative pairing ,\nthe scalar test derivative, and  are interval-integrable.\nIntegration by parts then gives the distributional time-test identity. Tests vanishing only\nat the final endpoint retain the initial boundary term. Replacing the trajectory by an\nalmost-everywhere equal Bochner  representative gives the identity used by the\nHilbert integrated weak formulation.\n\nUnder these hypotheses, pair the pointwise weak equation with a scalar time test and integrate.\nIntegration by parts changes\n into\n plus endpoint terms. A test vanishing at both\nends removes both terms; a test vanishing only at the final endpoint leaves\n. Almost-everywhere replacement of  does not change\nany Bochner integral, giving the completed-space identity.\n\nIn the opposite direction, the integrated identity determines a time\nderivative, and from there the Lions–Magenes theory applies to every\nintegrated weak solution.\n\nIf  is strongly measurable and essentially uniformly\nbounded, the time-tested integrated equation identifies the residualas an element of  and proves that it is the Gelfand weak time\nderivative of .  Consequently  almost everywhere.\nFor , the same theorem chain returns a continuous pivot\nrepresentative with left endpoint exactly ; the trace equality comes\nfrom the initial-endpoint test.\n\nUniform boundedness of  sends  to\n, so  is a valid Bochner class.\nMove the form term in the integrated equation to the right; the remaining\nidentity is exactly the Gelfand weak-derivative test formula for .\nLions–Magenes supplies the continuous pivot representative. Comparing the\ninitial boundary term in the integrated identity with its fundamental\ntheorem formula forces the left trace to equal .\n\nWith the derivative and the continuous pivot representative in hand, the\nexact energy identity is available, and coercivity together with a uniform\nbound on the forcing turns it into the estimate that the Galerkin\napproximations satisfy. Continuity of  on  is used only to\nmake a pointwise forcing\nan  class; the estimate itself uses the bound  alone.\n\nLet , let  be strongly measurable and essentially uniformly\nbounded, and let  be continuous on . Assume , ,\n, and  for every\n and . Every integrated weak solution admits a\ncontinuous pivot representative with the prescribed initial value and, for\nevery ,This is the final-solution counterpart of Evans's uniform Galerkin estimate.\nThe theorem also reconstructs an  weak derivative; endpoint\nvalues refer to the returned continuous representative.\n\nRecover the weak time derivative and Lions–Magenes representative from the\nintegrated identity, identify the initial endpoint, and combine the exact\npivot energy identity with coercivity and Young's inequality.\n\nFor two solutions with the same data the forcing cancels from the\ndifference, so no bound on  is needed and coercivity can be relaxed to\na Gårding inequality. The lower-order term  that\nthe inequality introduces into the energy balance is absorbed by an integral\nGrönwall argument on the pivot norm of the difference.\n\nLet  and let  be strongly measurable and essentially uniformly\nbounded. Assume, for all  and ,with  and . Two integrated weak solutions with the same\nforcing and initial datum are equal as  classes. This follows\nEvans's lower-order uniqueness argument; the unshifted form need not be\nnonnegative.\n\nApply the Lions–Magenes identity to the difference, discard the nonnegative\nenergy term, and use integral Grönwall on the squared pivot norm. Almost\neverywhere equality of included representatives then gives equality in\n by injectivity of .\n\nWith , keeping the differences of forcing and initial data, instead\nof setting them to zero, turns the same computation into a stability estimate.\n\nConsider two solutions for the same strongly measurable, essentially uniformly\nbounded form on , where . Assume the Gårding inequality with\n. Subtracting their integrated equations gives\nthe Gårding energy inequality for the continuous pivot-space difference, with both the\ndifference of initial data and the difference of  forcings visible.\nIn the strictly coercive case , a common initial datum gives the operator estimateThe zero-data specialization gives uniqueness, and the quantitative\ncontinuous-dependence form completes the Galerkin solution operator from\nregular to arbitrary  loads.\n\nSubtract the two integrated equations. The difference is an integrated weak\nsolution with initial datum  and forcing\n. Apply the Lions–Magenes energy identity, use the Gårding\nlower bound on the form, and estimate the dual forcing by Young's inequality.\nThe stated estimate retains the lower-order pivot integral; a further Grönwall\nstep can bound it. With common initial data and strict coercivity, integration\nand cancellation give\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Integrated Weak Evolution","header":"6.3.1.7.1. Integrated weak solutions","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Integrated-Weak-Evolution--Integrated-weak-solutions"}});